%tier 1.0.1
%title Payments API Runbook
%sum This runbook covers deploying, operating, and debugging the Payments API, the service that authorizes card payments and writes ledger entries for the checkout flow.
@overview Overview
? The Payments API is a stateless Go service fronted by an internal load balancer.
It talks to the card processor over mTLS, writes every authorization to the ledger database, and publishes settlement events to the `payments.settled` Kafka topic. It is owned by the Payments Platform team and is tier-1: an outage blocks all checkout traffic.
@architecture Architecture
? Requests enter through the API gateway, which handles authentication and rate limiting.
The Payments API validates the request, calls the card processor, then writes to Postgres in a single transaction. A separate worker, `settle-worker`, drains the outbox table and publishes events. Redis is used only for idempotency keys with a 24 hour TTL.
\> Note: the processor sandbox is shared across all teams and is frequently slow. Do not treat sandbox latency as a regression.
@environments Environments
? table: Environment,Region,URL,Replicas,Deploy policy
|environments[4]{Environment,Region,URL,Replicas,Deploy policy}
dev,us-east-1,https://payments.dev.internal,2,auto on merge
staging,us-east-1,https://payments.stg.internal,4,auto on tag
prod-us,us-east-1,https://payments.internal,12,manual approval
prod-eu,eu-west-1,https://payments.eu.internal,8,manual approval
@deploying Deploying
? Deploys to production are done by the on-call engineer or a release captain.
A deploy takes about 15 minutes end to end, including the canary.
1. Confirm CI is green on the release tag and that the staging soak has run for at least 30 minutes.
2. Post in #payments-releases with the tag and a link to the changelog.
3. Start the canary at 5 percent traffic:
$ deployctl canary payments-api --tag v2.41.0 --percent 5
4. Watch the canary dashboard for 10 minutes. Error rate must stay below 0.1 percent and p99 latency below 450 ms.
5. Promote to 100 percent:
$ deployctl promote payments-api --tag v2.41.0
6. Repeat for prod-eu after prod-us has been healthy for 30 minutes.
Always deploy prod-us before prod-eu. Never deploy on Fridays after 14:00 UTC or during a declared incident without approval from the incident commander.
@rolling-back Rolling back
? If the canary or the full rollout breaks the error budget, roll back immediately and investigate afterwards.
Rollbacks are safe because the service is stateless and database migrations are backward compatible for one release.
$ deployctl rollback payments-api --to previous
After rolling back, post in #payments-incidents, open an incident if customer impact lasted more than 5 minutes, and do not retry the deploy until the cause is understood. If the bad release included a migration, check the migrations section before the next attempt.
@migrations Migrations
? Schema changes ship separately from code.
A migration must be backward compatible with the previous release, because during a rollout both versions run at once. Add columns as nullable, backfill in batches, and only then add constraints in a later release.
! Never drop a column in the same release that stops using it.
! Never run a migration that takes a table lock for more than 2 seconds in prod.
! Always run the migration against a copy of staging data first.
```yaml
migration:
  lock_timeout: 2s
  batch_size: 5000
  sleep_between_batches: 200ms
```
@on-call-escalation On-call and escalation
? The primary on-call is paged for any sev-1 or sev-2 alert.
If the primary does not acknowledge within 10 minutes, the page escalates automatically.
|on_call_escalation[4]{Level,Role,Contact,Ack deadline}
1,Primary on-call,PagerDuty: payments-primary,10 min
2,Secondary on-call,PagerDuty: payments-secondary,10 min
3,Engineering manager,PagerDuty: payments-em,15 min
4,Director of Payments,"phone, see the leadership roster",15 min
@alerts Alerts
? table: Alert,Threshold,Severity,First action
|alerts[5]{Alert,Threshold,Severity,First action}
HighErrorRate,5xx above 1 percent for 5 min,sev-1,"Check the canary, roll back if a deploy is in flight"
HighLatencyP99,p99 above 800 ms for 10 min,sev-2,"Check processor status page, then DB connection saturation"
LedgerWriteFailures,any failed ledger write,sev-1,"Page the database on-call immediately, stop deploys"
OutboxLag,oldest outbox row older than 5 min,sev-2,Check settle-worker pods and Kafka broker health
IdempotencyRedisDown,Redis unavailable for 2 min,sev-3,"Service degrades safely, but duplicate charges are possible, so enable the kill switch"
@common-failures Common failures
? subtopics: Processor timeouts, Database connection saturation, Duplicate charges
> processor-timeouts Processor timeouts
> database-connection Database connection saturation
> duplicate-charges Duplicate charges
@processor-timeouts Processor timeouts
? Timeouts from the card processor usually come in bursts.
Check the processor status page first. If the processor is degraded, enable the fallback route with the feature flag `payments.fallback_processor`, which sends traffic to the secondary processor at a higher fee. Tell finance when the fallback is enabled for more than 30 minutes.
@database-connection Database connection saturation
? Symptoms are rising p99 latency and `too many clients` errors in the logs.
The pool is capped at 40 connections per replica. Do not raise the cap without checking the primary's `max_connections`. Scale replicas down before scaling up, because each replica holds a full pool.
@duplicate-charges Duplicate charges
? Duplicate charges indicate idempotency keys were lost, normally because Redis was flushed or unavailable.
Enable the kill switch `payments.reject_without_idempotency`, then identify affected orders from the ledger and hand the list to the support team for refunds.
@configuration Configuration
? Configuration is read from environment variables and the shared config service.
The important values are:
- processor
  - timeout_ms: 3000
  - retries: 2
  - mtls_cert: /etc/certs/processor.pem
- database
  - pool_size: 40
  - statement_timeout: 5s
- redis
  - idempotency_ttl: 24h
@security-rules Security rules
? rules: Never log full card numbers, CVVs, or processor tokens. Mask all but the last four digits.; Never paste production ledger data into tickets, chat, or...
! Never log full card numbers, CVVs, or processor tokens. Mask all but the last four digits.
! Never paste production ledger data into tickets, chat, or external tools.
! Always use the break-glass role for production database access, and note the ticket number.
! Do not share the processor mTLS certificate outside the Payments Platform team.
! Only the incident commander can approve a deploy during a declared incident.
